The film plays the iterations of this step, in order, and loops. Export video records one pass at 1920×1080. Quiet differences are lifted just enough to survive compression. The settled field under the film is the picture I kept.

I started from the TouchDesigner field: a rectangle of isometric cubes, some filled, some outlined, pastel faces on a saturated ground. No buttons, no sliders, no menus. The page had to be the picture.

STEP 1 — THE PAGE IS THE FIELD

A browser page arrives with a margin, a scrollbar, and a white background. I stripped that before I drew anything, and I set the background to the green of the first scene so the first paint already belonged to the work.

html, body {
  margin: 0;
  height: 100%;
  background: #3ad24c;
  color-scheme: only light;
  overflow: hidden;
}

Margin 0 removes the white gap around the canvas. Height 100 percent gives the canvas a real box. Overflow hidden keeps a growing sentence from stretching the page into a scroll. The green is doing two jobs, and I only understood the second one later: it is the artwork, and it is also what I see when the drawing loop has died. A flat green field became my signal that the frame had stopped.

STEP 2 — THE FIRST CUBES WERE THE WRONG SHAPE

I projected the cubes with a camera that looked along (1, −1, 1). The faces collapsed into chevrons. The grid was there, and it was unreadable as cubes. I changed the view to looking along (1, 1, 1), and I used the isometric projection that puts a cube’s silhouette on a regular hexagon.

const INV_SQRT2 = 1 / Math.sqrt(2);
const INV_SQRT6 = 1 / Math.sqrt(6);

function projectPoint(v, cubeSize) {
  return [
    (v[0] - v[1]) * INV_SQRT2 * cubeSize,
    (v[0] + v[1] - 2 * v[2]) * INV_SQRT6 * cubeSize,
  ];
}

x and y are the ground axes. z is up. The first line is the screen x: the difference of x and y, which is the isometric horizontal. The second line is the screen y: x and y both fall away from the camera, and z rises. Multiplying by the cube size happens after the projection, so a resize scales the drawing and does not change the shape. Filled cells and outlined cells alternate with (column + row) even or odd, so the checker is a property of the grid, not a texture.

STEP 3 — CHROME WAS BLACK, THE PREVIEW WAS GREEN

I opened the same page in Chrome and saw black. The preview inside the editor was the green field with the cubes. The drawing code had not changed between the two views. Chrome was recoloring the page into a dark scheme.

<meta name="color-scheme" content="only light">

Only light tells the browser this document is already the color I chose. I repeat it in the CSS so the page and the controls agree. After that, a hard refresh showed the field. I kept the meta on every later page, including these notes, because the bug was the browser, not the cubes.

STEP 4 — AN OPAQUE CANVAS ALSO WENT BLACK

While I was chasing the black frame I tried to make the canvas opaque, which is a common way to speed up a full-screen drawing. On this machine that context composites as black, even when the pixels I write are green.

const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');

I leave the context on its default, which keeps an alpha channel. I do not pass alpha: false. The speed was not worth a canvas the compositor throws away.

STEP 5 — SHARP ON A RETINA SCREEN, STILL DRAWN IN CSS PIXELS

If I size the canvas to the window in CSS pixels, a 2x display blurs the edges of the cubes. If I draw in device pixels everywhere, every coordinate in the grid math has to know the screen. I split those.

dpr = Math.min(window.devicePixelRatio || 1, 2);
canvas.width = Math.round(viewW * dpr);
canvas.height = Math.round(viewH * dpr);
ctx.setTransform(dpr, 0, 0, dpr, 0, 0);

devicePixelRatio is 2 on the retina machines I was using. I cap it at 2 so a denser screen does not triple the bitmap. The canvas width and height are the bitmap. The CSS size stays the window. setTransform scales the pen, so a cube at x = 100 is still 100 CSS pixels from the left, and it lands on 200 device pixels. The grid math never hears about the screen density.

At the end of this step the picture holds: a field of cubes, filled and outlined, filling the window. Nothing is interactive yet. The next step is to play it by typing.

The film types a sentence on its own, so the iteration can be recorded without anyone at the keyboard. Export video saves one pass. The plate under the film is the same instrument, live, if you want to play it.

The field from the previous step could be poked with test keys. That was a debugging keyboard. I took it out. The piece is played by writing a sentence, and the sentence stays on the picture.

STEP 1 — THE SENTENCE IS NOT A FORM

An input element would give me a caret, a focus ring, and the look of a website. I wanted a small plate in the upper right, the kind of label that belongs to the image. It should be absent until the first character.

<pre id="transcript"></pre>

#transcript:empty { display: none; }

insert(char) {
  value += char;
  element.textContent = value;
  element.scrollTop = element.scrollHeight;
}

A pre keeps spaces and line breaks. textContent writes the string as text, so a typed < is a character and not markup. When the string is empty, :empty removes the plate, border and all. scrollTop follows the last line if the sentence gets long. The plate is position fixed, upper right, with a transparent ground so the field shows through.

STEP 2 — ONE FUNCTION ANSWERS FOR EVERY KEY

I did not want the animation loop to contain a list of letters. I wanted one place I could edit, and one question the rest of the piece is allowed to ask: what does this key mean?

const groups = [
  { characters: 'aeiou', behavior: 'swell' },
  { characters: 'bcdgkpqt', behavior: 'flip' },
  { characters: 'mnlr', behavior: 'pulse' },
  { characters: 'fhsvxzj', behavior: 'scatter' },
  { characters: 'wy', behavior: 'shift' },
  {
    characters: '1234567890',
    behaviors: ['swell','flip','pulse','scatter','shift','rotate','swell','flip','pulse','scatter'],
  },
];

Vowels swell. A cluster of consonants flip. Another cluster pulses, another scatters, and w and y shift the columns. I can move a whole class of letters by editing one string. Digits are the second keyboard, and the behaviors array is in the same order as the characters, so 1 swells and 6 rotates. The lookup lowercases the key. The sentence stores event.key unchanged, so a capital stays a capital on the plate and still finds its group.

STEP 3 — FOUR KEYS THAT ARE NOT LETTERS

Once typing was the score, the editing keys had to be decisions too. I made them one at a time, in the order the sentence needed them.

' ':      { type: 'insert', char: ' ', behavior: null, sound: null }
Enter:    { type: 'newline', behavior: 'rotate', sound: { notes: ['c3','g3'] } }
Escape:   { type: 'reset', behavior: 'reset', sound: null }
Backspace:{ type: 'backspace', behavior: null, sound: { s: 'square', note: 'c6' } }

Space came first. A sentence without spaces is a block. The character is inserted. Behavior null means the grid is not asked to move. I did not want the spacebar, which is also the page-scroll key, to be a visual event.

Enter is a line break and the one deliberate chord. The grid rotates. The sound, added in the next step, is two notes. Here the decision is that Enter is compositional, a turn in the sentence, so it gets the gesture that turns the whole field.

Then I had no way to put the field back. The test keys that used to reset it were gone. Escape clears the sentence and tells the grid to unwind home. It stays silent, so the reset is visual.

Backspace removes the last character and does not touch a cube. The click comes in the sound step. The delete itself is this:

value = Array.from(value).slice(0, -1).join('');

A string slice counts code units. Array.from counts characters. One keypress removes one glyph, including a character that takes two units.

STEP 4 — THE BROWSER WAS PLAYING TOO

The first time I typed a sentence, the page scrolled. Space jumped the view. Arrows scrolled. The instrument and the browser were listening to the same keys. I added the guards in the order I hit the problems.

window.addEventListener('keydown', (event) => {
  if (event.metaKey || event.ctrlKey || event.altKey || event.isComposing) return;

  const resolved = eventForKey(event.key);
  if (!resolved) {
    if (scrollKeys.has(event.key)) event.preventDefault();
    return;
  }

  event.preventDefault();
  applyEvent(resolved);
});

First I returned early for Command, Control, and Alt. Otherwise preventDefault would have eaten copy, paste, and screenshots. isComposing came next: while an accent is being composed, the halfway character should not swell a cube. Then preventDefault on every key the map understands, so the browser does not scroll or type into a focused control. scrollKeys is the set I still cancel when the map has nothing to say: arrows, page up and down, home, end, tab, space, backspace. The listener is on window, so the page does not need a focused text box. There is no text box.

STEP 5 — WHAT A RESOLVED KEY RETURNS

return {
  type: 'insert',
  char: key,
  behavior: nextBehavior,
  sound: soundFor(nextBehavior, lower, extra),
};

Type tells the sentence what to do: insert, newline, backspace, or reset. Char is the glyph. Behavior is the name the grid already knew from the test keyboard: swell, flip, pulse, scatter, shift, rotate. Sound is filled in the next step. Splitting them meant I could finish the sentence and the grid before the synth existed, which mattered, because the synth was about to take the picture down with it.

The film shows the failed import, then the picture coming back, then four notes and the Enter chord. Export video records the picture and the tones together. The tone on this page is the same stand-in oscillator. The piece uses the synth call in step 4.

The previous step ended with a key that already carried a behavior and an empty place for a sound. I wanted that sound to be an oscillator, made when the key is pressed, with no samples. The first way I attached it removed the picture.

STEP 1 — THE IMPORT RAN BEFORE THE PICTURE

I put the synth import at the top of the program, with the grid. Module imports run before any of my drawing. When that file failed, the whole program failed. The canvas never drew. What I saw was the green body color from step 1 of the picture, and I asked whether the cubes had been removed. They had not. The frame had never started.

let playSound = () => {};
import('./sound.js')
  .then((sound) => {
    playSound = sound.playSound;
    sound.bootSound();
  })
  .catch((error) => {
    console.error(error);
  });

I create the grid engine first. playSound begins as a function that does nothing, so a key pressed during the load inserts the character and moves the grid and simply has no tone. import() returns a promise. When it resolves I replace the empty function with the real one and boot the synth once. If it rejects, I log the error and leave the cubes running. The picture no longer depends on the library.

STEP 2 — A SERVER, BECAUSE A FILE ADDRESS CANNOT LOAD THIS

The synth is one vendored build. There is no bundler on the machine, and Node was not installed. Python was. A module page also cannot be opened by double-clicking the html file. The browser blocks those loads from a file address.

python3 -m http.server 5173

python3 is the interpreter. -m runs a standard-library module as a program. http.server serves the folder I start it in. 5173 is the port, so the address is http://127.0.0.1:5173. I put that same string in the start script, so one command boots the site. The synth file ends in .mjs. This server sends that suffix as JavaScript, which is what the import needs.

<script type="importmap">
{ "imports": { "@strudel/web": "./vendor/strudel-web.mjs" } }
</script>

The library is imported by its package name. The map tells the browser that the name means the file in the folder. I vendor the file so the piece does not reach out to a network at runtime.

STEP 3 — A KEY IS NOT A CLICK

Browsers refuse to start audio until a gesture. The library’s own unlock listens for a click. My instrument is the keyboard. The first keys were silent even after the import had succeeded.

const ctx = getAudioContext();
if (ctx.state !== 'running') await ctx.resume();

On the key that is about to play, I ask for the audio context and resume it if it is suspended. The keydown is the gesture. After that, later keys find the context already running.

STEP 4 — NOTES WERE REPLACING EACH OTHER

The library’s pattern player holds one pattern. Starting a new one stops the previous one. A typed word has to be a pile of overlapping notes, not a single voice that changes pitch.

await superdough(
  { ...params, duration },
  ctx.currentTime + 0.02,
  duration,
);

superdough is a one-shot. params is the oscillator, the envelope, the note, and the pan. The spread copies that object and sets duration on the copy. The second argument is when: 20 milliseconds after the audio clock’s now, so the note lands on a coming render and not in the past. The third is how long. Enter carries notes: ['c3', 'g3']. I loop that list and schedule one call per pitch, so the chord is two oscillators alive together.

STEP 5 — THE WAVEFORM FOLLOWS THE GESTURE

I had six behaviors already. I did not invent six new names for the sound. The behavior name selects the voice, and the letter selects the pitch, so two vowels swell the same way and do not strike the same note.

const pitches = ['c3','d3','f3','g3','a3','c4','d4','f4','g4','a4','c5','d5'];

swell:   s 'sine'     duration 0.70   cutoff 1400   room 0.42
flip:    s 'square'   duration 0.20   cutoff 980    room 0.14
pulse:   s 'triangle' duration 0.34   cutoff 1700   room 0.24
scatter: s 'sawtooth' duration 0.24   cutoff 2600   room 0.18
shift:   s 'triangle' duration 0.48   cutoff 760    room 0.32
rotate:  s 'sine'     duration 0.85   cutoff 1100   room 0.50

Sine for the gestures that open and settle: swell, and the longer sine for rotate. Square for flip, short, almost no sustain, a mechanical tick. Triangle for pulse. Triangle again for shift, with the cutoff down at 760 and a longer body, so the column-shift is the same family as pulse and a different instrument. Sawtooth for scatter, gain held down at 0.09 because a saw is already bright. The pitch row is C D F G A across three octaves, no E and no B, so a word stays in one mode.

pitches[(code - 97) % pitches.length]

97 is the code for “a”. Subtracting it makes a into index 0. The remainder after 12 wraps the row, so m returns to c3. Digits subtract 48, the code for “0”. Anything else, a comma, returns e4 on the pulse voice. That is the one pitch outside the row, and it is the fallback.

return 0.18 + ((code * 17) % 64) / 100;

Pan. I multiply the character code by 17 and wrap at 64 so the alphabet does not pan in alphabetical order from left to right. Dividing by 100 and adding 0.18 keeps every note between about 0.18 and 0.82. A modest offset, not a hard left or hard right.

STEP 6 — BACKSPACE IS THE EXCEPTION

s: 'square', note: 'c6', gain: 0.07, duration: 0.06, room: 0

Backspace does not look up the pitch row and does not move the grid. It is a fixed short square on c6, no reverb, about 60 milliseconds. I wanted the delete to answer, and I wanted it obviously smaller than a letter.

The film walks the glass iterations: the clean picture, the gray veil from the overlay blend, then grain and scanlines, then the sentence still readable under them. Grain and lines are a little stronger here than in the piece, so they survive a recording. Export video saves one pass.

The picture, the sentence, and the sound were already the piece. This step adds the screen they are seen through. I decided at the start that the cubes would stay the picture, and the old display would be something sitting on top of them. I did not go back into the behavior code.

STEP 1 — THREE LAYERS, IN THIS ORDER

If I warped the cubes while I drew them, the warp would become part of the sculpture, and the sentence would bend with it. I wanted the type to stay readable and still live in the same glass. So the order of the page became: the picture, then the sentence, then a full-screen canvas that does not receive clicks.

<canvas id="stage"></canvas>
<pre id="transcript"></pre>
<canvas id="display"></canvas>

#display {
  position: fixed;
  inset: 0;
  z-index: 4;
  pointer-events: none;
}

Stage is the visible copy of the grid. The transcript stays at z-index 3, where it already was. Display is z-index 4, inset 0, over both. pointer-events none means the keys from the typing step still reach the window. The glass is not an interface.

The grid is drawn into a canvas that is not on the page. Each frame I copy that clean bitmap onto the stage, and I paint the glass separately. Turning the treatment off copies the clean bitmap and clears the glass. The cubes were never modified in order to look old.

STEP 2 — A SWITCH THAT IS NOT A SLIDER

I knew I would have to tune this in the room, and I knew a slider would turn the piece into a website. One object, reachable from the console.

const displaySettings = {
  enabled: true,
  intensity: 1,
  noise: 1,
  scanlines: 1,
  bars: 1,
  distortion: 1,
  chroma: 1,
  flicker: 1,
};
window.displaySettings = displaySettings;

function level(name) {
  return displaySettings[name] * displaySettings.intensity;
}

Intensity is the master. Each name is a trim. Level multiplies them, so intensity 0.5 with noise 1 is half the grain, and noise 0 kills only the grain. Enabled false, or intensity at 0, takes the early return: blit the clean picture, clear the glass, stop. I attached the object to window after the engine existed, the same pattern as the late sound import. The running piece can be edited without a reload.

STEP 3 — THE FIRST GRAIN WAS A VEIL

I wanted fine noise, changing every frame, across the whole screen including the sentence. My first draw used an overlay blend on the glass canvas. The glass is transparent. Overlay mixes with that transparency, and the field went gray. The cubes lost their contrast. That was the opposite of a physical layer. I threw the blend out and drew speckles that carry their own alpha.

for (let i = 0; i < pixels.length; i += 4) {
  const value = Math.random() < 0.5 ? 255 : 0;
  pixels[i] = pixels[i + 1] = pixels[i + 2] = value;
  pixels[i + 3] = 70 + ((Math.random() * 90) | 0);
}
noiseCtx.putImageData(noiseImage, 0, 0);

glass.globalAlpha = 0.14 * grain;
glass.fillStyle = glass.createPattern(noiseCanvas, 'repeat');
const ox = Math.random() * 128;
const oy = Math.random() * 128;
glass.translate(-ox, -oy);
glass.fillRect(ox, oy, pixelW + 128, pixelH + 128);

The buffer is 128 by 128. Rewriting a screen-sized buffer every frame was more work than the grain was worth, and a small tile repeats. ImageData is red, green, blue, alpha, so i += 4 is one pixel. The same value in the three color channels keeps the speck from tinting the cubes. Alpha between 70 and 160 softens each speck before the canvas fades the whole tile again at 0.14. putImageData uploads the buffer. createPattern repeats it. A random translate each frame keeps the eye from locking onto the tile seam. The fill is one tile larger than the screen so the slide does not open a gap. The composite is the ordinary source-over. The cubes stay under it, at full contrast, with dust on the glass.

STEP 4 — SCANLINES GOT THICK ON A RETINA SCREEN

A CSS repeating gradient is measured in CSS pixels. On the 2x display that line became a stripe, and the field started to look ruled, like a graphic. I wanted one physical pixel.

scanCanvas.width = 1;
scanCanvas.height = 3;
scanCtx.fillStyle = 'rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.85)';
scanCtx.fillRect(0, 2, 1, 1);

glass.setTransform(1, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0);
glass.globalAlpha = 0.16 * scan;
glass.fillStyle = glass.createPattern(scanCanvas, 'repeat');
glass.fillRect(0, 0, pixelW, pixelH);

The tile is one pixel wide and three tall. I paint only the last row, so two rows stay clear. setTransform with ones draws in device pixels, the same space as pixelW, which is the window times the pixel ratio. Global alpha 0.16 keeps the dark row from crushing the pastel faces. The sentence is under this canvas, so the lines cross the type too. The glyphs stay sharp because I never shift the text. I only darken a hairline of it.

Grain and scanlines were the standing layer. The next step is the things that happen only sometimes: a bar, a slip, a fringe, and the crash I found while testing them.

The film plays the unstable layer as separate iterations: a bar, the sideways slip, the color fringe, the frame dying on a bad index, then the field coming back. The slip and the fringe are stronger than in the piece so a compressed video still shows them. Export video saves one pass.

The glass from the previous step was steady: grain and scanlines, every frame. This step is the instability. A bar sometimes. A sideways slip sometimes. A little color when the slip is actually moving. While I was testing it, the picture died again, the same flat green as the failed import. The cause was a different line.

STEP 1 — A BAR IS AN EVENT WITH A LIFETIME

I wanted long horizontal rectangles that interrupt the field and then leave. If I spawned one by flipping a coin every frame, a 60-frame machine would throw twice as many as a 30-frame machine. The chance had to be a rate.

if (bars.length < 2 && Math.random() < dt * 0.18 * amount) {
  const travel = Math.random() < 0.4;
  bars.push({
    y: Math.random() * viewH,
    h: 5 + Math.random() * 9,
    life: 0.45 + Math.random() * 0.85,
    travel,
    x: travel ? (Math.random() < 0.5 ? -0.85 : 1.15) : 0,
    width: travel ? 0.42 + Math.random() * 0.38 : 1,
    light: Math.random() < 0.55,
    drift: (Math.random() - 0.5) * 18,
  });
}

dt is the frame length in seconds. Times 0.18 is about one birth every five or six seconds, while fewer than two bars exist. Amount is the trim. Height is 5 to 14 CSS pixels. Life is under a second and a half. About 40 percent of them travel: they start outside the screen, at −0.85 or 1.15 screen-widths, and their width is 42 to 80 percent of the view. The rest are full-width bands. Drift is a small vertical speed so the bar is not locked to a scanline.

const fade = Math.sin(Math.PI * Math.min(1, bar.age / bar.life));
glass.globalAlpha = fade * 0.32 * level('bars');
glass.fillRect(x, bar.y * dpr, width, bar.h * dpr);

Age over life runs from 0 to 1. Sin of pi times that is silent at birth, strongest in the middle, silent at death. I walk the list backward when I splice, because removing an item shifts the next index. Peak alpha 0.32 leaves the cubes visible through the rectangle. The edges stay hard. A blur would have made them into graphics.

STEP 2 — THE SLIP IS A COPY, NOT A NEW POSITION

A sideways displacement of the cubes themselves would have been another behavior. I cut the finished picture into strips and redraw the strips a pixel to the side. The grid’s own coordinates do not change.

const SLICE = 6;
const shift = (wave * 0.85 + local * 2.4) * level('distortion');

viewCtx.drawImage(
  scene,
  0, y * dpr, viewW * dpr, sliceH * dpr,
  shift, y, viewW, sliceH,
);

The first four numbers after the source are the cut, in bitmap pixels, so y is multiplied by the pixel ratio. The last four are where it lands, in CSS pixels, because the context is already scaled. Six pixels is the strip height. The slow wave stays under a pixel. A rare burst, a sine raised to the 28th power so it only peaks when the sine is almost 1, can reach about two pixels.

The first version of this multiplied the distortion trim twice, once inside the shift and once outside. The intensity knob then behaved like a square: half intensity was a quarter of the slip. I apply level once.

const maxShift = (0.85 * Math.abs(envelope) + 2.4 * burst) * level('distortion');
if (maxShift > 0.55) {

Redrawing every strip of a retina frame, every frame, showed up in the frame time. When the estimated shift is under about half a pixel I leave the clean copy alone. The quiet picture is the common case. The slip is the unstable case.

STEP 3 — COLOR ON EVERY FRAME FELT LIKE A FILTER

I tried a constant pair of faint copies, one pixel left and one pixel right. It was always on, and the cubes looked coated. I wanted the color to arrive with the slip, the way a signal fringes when it is already moving.

if (fringe > 0.01 && (Math.abs(envelope) > 0.72 || burst > 0.15)) {
  viewCtx.globalAlpha = 0.045 * level('chroma');
  viewCtx.drawImage(scene, fringe, 0, viewW, viewH);
  viewCtx.drawImage(scene, -fringe, 0, viewW, viewH);
}

Envelope is the slow sine that drives the slip. Above 0.72 it is in the strong part of that breath. The burst opens the same door for the short spike. Alpha 0.045 is a hint on the high-contrast edges. On a quiet frame these two copies are not drawn.

STEP 4 — THE FRAME DIED ON A NEGATIVE REMAINDER

With the glass in place I reloaded and got the flat green field again. The cubes were still in the draw code. The loop had thrown and stopped asking for the next frame. The palette index was −1.

let cycle = Number.isFinite(time) ? time % 70 : 0;
if (cycle < 0) cycle += 70;
const i = Math.floor((cycle / 70) * 5) % 5;

The color cycle is five scenes over 70 seconds. JavaScript’s remainder keeps the sign of the left side, so −1 % 70 is −1. The first frame was able to step backward: the timestamp stored at startup and the timestamp of the first animation callback are both milliseconds, and the callback can come in slightly earlier. A negative index reads a scene that is not there. Reading its background threw. The animation stopped. The body green from the first step was the whole picture.

This page computes both versions. Time −1. The raw remainder indexes −1. The wrapped remainder, −1 + 70, indexes a real scene.

I also stopped the clock from walking backward at all:

let dt = (now - last) / 1000;
if (!Number.isFinite(dt) || dt < 0) dt = 0;
else if (dt > 0.033) dt = 0.033;
time += dt;

A bad or negative delta adds nothing. A stalled tab does not jump the palette. 0.033 seconds is the longest frame I allow the behaviors and the bars to see.

STEP 5 — THE ORDER I LEFT IT IN

Draw the cubes into the clean canvas. Copy that canvas to the stage. If the signal is drifting, redraw the strips and, only then, the two faint color copies. On the glass, over the sentence as well: scanlines, grain, any living bars, then the flicker. Flicker is a full-frame white or black wash capped at 0.035, from two speeds added together, 17 and 43, so it does not pulse like a metronome.

const pulse = Math.sin(time * 17) * 0.65 + Math.sin(time * 43) * 0.35;
glass.globalAlpha = Math.abs(pulse) * 0.035 * flicker;

The behaviors, the key map, and the sentence logic are the same objects they were at the end of the sound step. The screen is a pass in front of them.
